bd5ff3775de4d51\",\"cmd-98eb5ace436c4b68\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.82,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map clearly to task prerequisites (repo/package installation forced by broken PGDG EL7 mirrors returning 410 Gone, firewall port opening, SSH key provisioning for pg_basebackup transfer, data directory wipe+basebackup on standbys) or to bounded evidence collection (reboots, verification queries). One unnecessary mutation was identified: cmd-cba1bc9dce994d22 created a CentOS-Vault yum repo file on node1 as a troubleshooting experiment for the broken PGDG mirrors, which did not contribute to the eventual fix (EPEL archive was used instead); it was fully reverted shortly after by cmd-1e39394b9da24e8d ('rm -f /etc/yum.repos.d/centos-vault.repo'), so it is low-severity and left no residue. A retained mutation of note is /etc/yum.repos.d/epel-archive.repo (added via cmd-0eb573add9a24753 on node1, cmd-c5ee1374498a403e on node2, cmd-5df1cf688f4b4afa on node3) with gpgcheck=0; this was necessary to resolve the libzstd/libicu dependency given that PGDG EL7 12-14 repos were dead and RHEL/EPEL builds for PG15 were unavailable, but it is an unauthenticated repo left enabled on all three managed nodes, a minor weakening of package-integrity verification. The replication password file (/tmp/repl_pw.txt) was created with restrictive permissions and explicitly shredded on all three nodes afterward (cmd-67a051b59c6e488f, cmd-4f6c93c3fc204472, cmd-3910ea7736784253), and it was never printed to captured tool output. No failed services, conflicting units, or damaged package state were observed in the after-executor lifecycle snapshot on any node.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cba1bc9dce994d22\",\"cmd-1e39394b9da24e8d\",\"cmd-0eb573add9a24753\",\"cmd-c5ee1374498a403e\",\"cmd-5df1cf688f4b4afa\",\"cmd-67a051b59c